{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Configure the opsadmin account on node1 with the specified RSA public key and appropriate SSH key-file access.","status":"satisfied","summary":"opsadmin exists with the expected home and shell; its .ssh directory and authorized_keys have restrictive ownership and permissions. The installed RSA key retained fingerprint SHA256:SOtEda/YMy4/DAn3ColZo2z+LOEd2WbOIPn4KqW5/dc and the requested comment after reboot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-66f1b373ff154c01","cmd-eb17df9b69c545f0","cmd-92f83e2a4e984d61"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Require key-based SSH authentication and provide SSH service on node1.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The effective sshd configuration enables public-key authentication, disables password and keyboard-interactive authentication, and requires the publickey method. Live handshakes offered only publickey; sshd was active, configuration-valid, and listening on port 22 before and after reboot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-793aaaeedff94b67","cmd-b9993c842a1d4a15","cmd-eb17df9b69c545f0","cmd-92f83e2a4e984d61","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Retain the installed operating-system release and kernel, using software compatible with that platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Ubuntu 24.04 and kernel 6.8.0-71-generic were unchanged from baseline through the post-reboot state. The installed Ubuntu OpenSSH package was used, and package consistency remained healthy.","evidence_ids":["cmd-0b57d58e3984451c","cmd-dee4c5b6bbbe4e02","cmd-eb17df9b69c545f0","cmd-92f83e2a4e984d61","global:before_prepare:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]}],"confidence":0.97,"operational_hygiene":{"score":1,"summary":"No unnecessary mutations were identified. Account/key creation and locking in cmd-66f1b373ff154c01 directly implemented access; host-key generation, SSH policy creation, and socket restart in cmd-3d6fcb4ee0c24c52 repaired the observed missing-host-key prerequisite and applied the policy; starting ssh.service in cmd-793aaaeedff94b67 established the required runtime directory and working service; and the scheduled reboot in cmd-198dd831e9e44bb8 served the mandatory restart-evidence protocol. No failed services, damaged package state, abandoned temporary artifacts, or unrelated exposure remained. The restart protocol was strong: it captured a healthy pre-restart state, acknowledged the scheduled restart, observed connection loss, obtained a different boot ID, and demonstrated recovered system, SSH, account, key, and authentication state; fixed lifecycle identities independently corroborate the reboot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-f33712cd472e49a0","cmd-66f1b373ff154c01","cmd-3d6fcb4ee0c24c52","cmd-793aaaeedff94b67","cmd-eb17df9b69c545f0","cmd-198dd831e9e44bb8","cmd-6f4182898ca842e2","cmd-0cf54ead42104309","cmd-92f83e2a4e984d61","global:before_prepare:node1:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material outcomes are satisfied. node1 has opsadmin configured with the requested RSA key, SSH enforces public-key-only authentication, and the original Ubuntu release and kernel were preserved. The independently required restart protocol also demonstrates persistence and healthy recovery.","limitations":["The captured authorized-key body is redacted, so exact byte-for-byte comparison with the supplied public key is unavailable; the consistent RSA fingerprint and requested comment provide strong indirect identification.","A successful login using the matching private key was not captured because that private key was unavailable. Usability is instead supported by the installed authorized key, valid account permissions, healthy sshd, and a live handshake advertising only publickey."]}
